[quote="Jeysie"]

&nbsp;
<div class="bb-quote"><b>Chaosmancer wrote:</b><br /><blockquote class="bb-quote-body">As far as I am aware, they are entirely seperated from each other.&nbsp;The Multiverse in all it&#39;s infinite glory still exists, it is just that the various &quot;rivers&quot; cannot cross or meet or flood into each other.&nbsp;However, there is something odd with the way they described Haka in this last episode. They made it sound like he was not in the Tactics timeline, and then later he was during the events of Prime War. This makes it sound like somehow he broke the new rules and traveled between timelines and realities.&nbsp;They may have just mispoken or I may have just misheard or misinterpretted, but, like you said, nothing prevents us from breaking this rule at anytime we feel neccesary as GMs
&nbsp;
</blockquote></div>
You missed part of the episode I think. Spoilers:&nbsp; -snip-&nbsp; No actual rule-breaking.I am now curious about what will be the end results of what you (I think it was you) said about Argent&#39;s new personal goal in the RPG, though.

[/quote]

&nbsp;

It was me who mentioned that in the Starter Kit Argent Adept mentioned that the Prime Wardens broke up after the loss of Haka. He&#39;s caring for Akash&#39;Flora and it seems part of the Starter Kit story is getting the Prime Wardens back together again, to deal with some new threat. IT involved SPider-Bots that used a lot of different tech. And by a lot of different tech I mean tech from every major source that I am aware of. Bunker, Tachyon, Unity, Omnitron, Baron Blade, Voss, FILTER, and I think Revocorp if memory serves me.

&nbsp;

We also know that part of getting the team together is going to involve the Femae version of HAka fighting Tempest. And Tempest doesn&#39;t rejoin, perhaps only if she joins but that seems signifigant. But it was almost mentioned in passing by our DM so it is hard to know for certain how much is revealed in the Starter Kit.

&nbsp;

As for not actually breaking the &quot;no dimension hopping rule&quot; it is very possible that you are right, but wouldn&#39;t it be odd for Haka to lay low for 5+ years in the tactics timeline, allowing so many people to be injured and hurt in the events that follow, without showing up on anyones radar?

&nbsp;

We know he isn&#39;t in Broken City, and if we take their original production schedule as the timeline for Tactics it is quite a long while before Prime War and Haka showing up. It seems exceedingly strange to me that he would just be laying low and not doing anything of importance. That isn&#39;t Haka.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

[quote="cnranger"]

&nbsp;Or maybe the mist gate spit him out at exactly the Prime War arc skipping over the time difference?

[/quote]

&nbsp;

That&#39;s an interesting idea. We do know there is this Mist in the Tactics Timeline that is making things vanish and disappear.

&nbsp;

Let us assume that the Gates are more built like tunnels. There is a section where you are in neither reality, but instead between them. Haka is mid-journey, or trying to run and jump back thru the Gate when it closes. He&#39;s smashed in the middle where he is between realities instead of in any single reality.

&nbsp;

Most people would die, but Haka is &quot;quantumly entangled&quot; or however you would phrase it, and cannot die as long as his counter part is still alive and therefore he is simply floating and existing between realities.

&nbsp;

Eventually, when someone tries something new to combat this mist, it results in Haka getting spit out into the Tactics timeline, right in time for Prime War. This works as well as a narrative retcon, Haka was lost and &quot;gotten rid of&quot; by the writers of the RPG timeline, but the Tactics writers found a way to bring him back for ther story that does not break any of the new rules, just bends them in half.

&nbsp;

I like it. Headcannon for now until disproven or displaced.

&nbsp;

[quote="scifantasy"]

But as a matter of &quot;the canonical story,&quot; what&#39;s the status?This is turning into a specific question for the podcast...

[/quote]

&nbsp;

Only two people have ever traveled directly in time in the Sentinels of the Multiverse Story. Chrono-Ranger and La Capitan/Commodora. Everyone else reality hopped.

&nbsp;

Officially, reality hopping is no longer possible, because that was only possible because of Oblivaeon and Oblivaeon no longer exists in a capacity that allows for that to happen. That is the canon answer as far as we the consumers have been told.

&nbsp;

As for Time Travel, Chrono-Ranger can no longer travel like he used to. And instead works with manipulating Time and traveling in short bursts, like no more than an hour or so, as far as I understand from the future section of Chrono-Ranger.

&nbsp;

I do not know the fate of La Comodora or La Capitan, so I cannot comment.

&nbsp;

However, since what they were doing is seperate from traveling to different realities, it is reasonable to assume that time travel is possible. However, it would be incredibly risky and perhaps locked down by some force. Because, if it is possible to jump back in time the Oblivaeon fight could be affected. A change could end up turning the fight at a crucial moment and allowing Oblivaeon to win, which would cause massive problems for the Multiverse if it were to happen.

&nbsp;

This is actually a good spot for La Commodora, acting as the Gate Keeper for Time. Preventing or closely monitoring anyone who might travel back to before Oblivaeon so that the timeline is not disrupted and the events unfold like they are supposed to.

&nbsp;

Or, because of the Death of Oblivaeon and it itself probably becoming a massive fixed point in time, it might have become impossible to travel back before that. There is simply too much chronal interference and energy released during that battle that it would be like taking a sailboat thru the Red Spot of Jupiter, you&#39;ll be smashed to pieces and killed nearly instantly, so it is a really really bad idea.

&nbsp;

Not sure which of these ideas I prefer.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

[quote="bluedarky"]

&nbsp;
<div class="bb-quote"><b>Chaosmancer wrote:</b><br /><blockquote class="bb-quote-body">But it was almost mentioned in passing by our DM so it is hard to know for certain how much is revealed in the Starter Kit.&nbsp;As for not actually breaking the &quot;no dimension hopping rule&quot; it is very possible that you are right, but wouldn&#39;t it be odd for Haka to lay low for 5+ years in the tactics timeline, allowing so many people to be injured and hurt in the events that follow, without showing up on anyones radar?&nbsp;We know he isn&#39;t in Broken City, and if we take their original production schedule as the timeline for Tactics it is quite a long while before Prime War and Haka showing up. It seems exceedingly strange to me that he would just be laying low and not doing anything of importance. That isn&#39;t Haka.
&nbsp;
</blockquote></div>
From my POV it sounds exactly like Haka, he probably realised the state the world was in and wandered around to help fix things in areas most of the world forgot, Oblivaeon&#39;s attack on Earth probably caused massive widespread damage not just in places we know, whilst places like Megaopolis probably received massive amounts of public and private funding to fix the damage what about places in Africa, China, New Zealand...Haka is the sort who sees violence as a last resort, and would relish a chance to use his strength and ability to take large amounts of damage to help people, like helping at underfunded construction sites carrying stuff and doing the more dangerous work to prevent others from hurting themselves. &nbsp;He probably only shows up for Prime War because Argent Adept sought him out to help.

[/quote]

&nbsp;

That&#39;s a good point, but I guess it speaks to my misunderstanding the... presentation, of the various heroes.

&nbsp;

Haka is a major hero in the Sentinels world. The implication that he simply appeared in Tactics makes it sound like no one knew where he was. If he was in Africa or New Zealand helping, my assumption was that people would notice.

&nbsp;

After all, he&#39;s a major hero, it&#39;d be like Legacy working a soup kitchen in Cuba, everyone is going to know Legacy is down there, he&#39;s too famous. And I&#39;m used to considering the heroes in terms of their fame and importance outside of the world of comics instead of considering how well known ther would be in universe.

&nbsp;

Haka has saved multiple cities just from the Letters Page story, he fought Oblivaeon, wouldn&#39;t people know his face? But, actually, probably not. And how would they identify him? He&#39;s a massive man, but there are many very large men in the world.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

So, I stll don&#39;t think that&#39;s the way things end up shaking out, but I acknowledge it is a possible explanation
